bootable install diskettes
==========================

	make bootdisks

at the top of the tree builds three, here:

	d8ss.img	8 inch, single sided	77 cyl, 1 head,  15 x 512	1125 blocks
	d8ds.img	8 inch, double sided	77 cyl, 2 heads, 15 x 512	2250 blocks
	d5ds.img	5 1/4, double sided	40 cyl, 2 heads, 10 x 512	 760 blocks

They are build output, regenerated from the tree the way a .vol is, and
.gitignore drops them.  create_bootdisk is the script that makes them;
manifest reads one back.  Nothing here is the distributed standalone:
see REGEN.md in ../hdinstall for what patching that costs and for what
is left open there.

What one carries, without booting it:

	./manifest d5ds.img

lists every path, its size and its device numbers, and the blocks used
and free.  It reads the image, not the build script, so it is the
answer to "what is actually on this diskette", and the block counts
below come from it.

The five inch single sided medium is deliberately absent.  194 K is not
a running system, and a diskette that can only be a data carrier is not
worth a media row.

what one is
-----------

A whole small micronix, on one diskette, whose root is the diskette.
The loader is in the sectors in front of the filesystem and the
filesystem behind it, and one struct dlabel at byte 256 of block 0
describes both - so the diskette says what it is rather than the name it
is reached by.  A reader with the file in its hands and no device number
at all can mount it.  See docs/DISKLABEL.md for the label,
and src/micronix/stand/boot/README for the loader.

To boot one:

	cd ../../src/hwsim/d1
	./d1 -B djdma -5 <the d5ds image>

The bare filename of an eight inch image goes on the eight inch port;
the five inch one needs -5.  Both are unskewed raw images - the DJ's
alternate sectoring bit is clear - which is what the loader and the host
tools both read.  -B djdma is the boot device; the monitor rom and the
level 1 rom the chain starts from are the simulator's own defaults, and
-b and -M name others.

what is on it, and why so little
--------------------------------

The install diskette is one article for every controller: it carries
hdinit, the parameterized install script, and the level 2 + level 3
boot of each controller.  hdinit takes the controller as its argument,
so one diskette installs onto any of them.  It is not a tool bag -
hdinit mkfs's a hard disk, mounts it on /b and copies the running
system across with cptree - so what has to fit is the smallest micronix
that can do that.  The five inch medium is the tight one:

	/micronix	the kernel, 128 K (131072 bytes), 257 blocks
			with its indirect block
	/boot		the four controllers' level 2 + level 3 boots,
			about 4k each - bootmw.bin, bootide.bin,
			bootncr.bin and boothdca.bin - 32 blocks
	/hdinit		the install script, 1619 bytes
	/bin		the fourteen commands an install needs:
			sh rm mkfs mount umount chmod cptree mknod
			setdev ln sync echo format halt.  sh is the real
			Bourne shell, because hdinit uses case and
			parameters; the old csh-like sh is not here
	/dev		every node in ../../src/micronix/dev/devlist,
			including kmem; the directory is three blocks
	/etc		the config and init, thirteen files
	/b /f /tmp	the mount points the install uses, and a scratch
			directory

The five inch image uses 589 of its 760 blocks and leaves 171 free.
The eight inch carries the same set in 626 of 2250 on the double sided
medium, and in 599 of 1125 on the single sided one.  Run manifest for
the live numbers: the binaries and the init change, and the free count
changes with them.

fsck is not on the install diskette.  An install makes a new
filesystem; checking the one it just made is not a step that can repair
anything, and the block it costs is better spent on the formatter.
label is not there either: format writes the label and its prototype
table.  The tools for repairing an existing hard disk are on the
maintenance diskette, below.

the formatter: format
---------------------

The install diskette carries one formatter binary where it used to
carry hdformat, mwformat and scsiformat.  format reads the controller
from the device node - the high byte of the node's device number is the
major - so the same command serves the HD-DMA, the HDCA, the 5380 SCSI
adapter, the IDE card and the DJ-DMA floppy.  For the four hard disk
controllers the install path passes the drive's whole-disk slice and
its shape:

	format -m m40 /dev/hd0c			the HD-DMA
	format -m m26 /dev/hdac			the HDCA
	format -c 392 -h 16 -s 63 /dev/scsi0c	the SCSI target
	format -c 392 -h 16 -s 63 /dev/ide0c	the IDE card

-m names a model from the controller's own table: m5, m10, m16, m32
and m40, and the xt, st, ms, tm, q, wren and mp MFM drives on the
HD-DMA; m10, m20 and m26 on the HDCA.  m10 is the Seagate ST-412 on one
and the Discus M10 on the other, which is why the table is chosen by
the controller and not by the name alone.  -c, -h and -s are cylinders,
heads and 512-byte blocks per track, and override a model or a label.
The HD-DMA also takes -P precomp, -l lowcur, -d step and -k skew, and
-t/-e for a single cylinder or head; -p is the HDCA's header probe.
An option the controller has no use for is an error, not ignored.

format writes the label as well as the medium: the geometry, the first
sector the controller numbers from - one on the HDCA, zero everywhere
else - the drive's timing on the HD-DMA, and the prototype slice table.
Prototype a is the biggest filesystem that fits, b is 1M of swap, and d
through h are further filesystems.  A low-level format has just erased
the label with everything else it wrote, so the label is written after;
a format that writes only headers, or a card with no format command at
all, keeps a carved table that was already there.

The IDE card has no format command in this tree: its format writes the
label and the prototype table and nothing else, so the drive must
already be one the card understands.  The other three lay the medium
down.  See format(1).

install, from the diskette
--------------------------

Boot it, wait for the shell's "#", and run hdinit with the controller
and, if the drive does not already carry a label, the format options
for its geometry:

	sh hdinit hdcdma -m m40
	sh hdinit ide -c 392 -h 16 -s 63
	sh hdinit scsi -c 392 -h 16 -s 63
	sh hdinit hdca -m m26

The controller is hdcdma, ide, scsi (or ncr) or hdca.  Everything after
it is passed to format with the drive's whole-disk node; with no
options, format is not run and the drive must already carry a label.
The controller is also what picks the device, the kernel major and the
boot mkfs writes below slice a:

	hdcdma	/dev/hd0a	/dev/hd0c	3	/boot/bootmw.bin
	ide	/dev/ide0	/dev/ide0c	4	/boot/bootide.bin
	scsi	/dev/scsi0	/dev/scsi0c	5	/boot/bootncr.bin
	hdca	/dev/hda	/dev/hdac	1	/boot/boothdca.bin

hdinit then mkfs's slice a with the controller's boot (-i) and -f,
mounts it on /b, and copies the whole running system across with
cptree.  It makes /b/dev/root and /b/dev/swap the drive's slice a and
b nodes - minor 0 and 32 under the controller's major - and points
/b/micronix at them with setdev.  It removes the install's own files
from the copy - hdinit, the four boots, the plain signon - and links
the signon2 the diskette carries as /b/etc/signon.  Then it unmounts,
syncs and prints "Disk initialization complete."

The boot is written by mkfs from the /boot file hdinit names, so the
drive boots when hdinit is done; there is no host hardboot step.  The
boots live in /boot and not in the root because the floppy's own level
3 offers every large file in the root and would stop at "File to
boot:".

cptree is copying a whole filesystem through a simulated Z80, so give
it time.

the maintenance diskette
------------------------

	./create_bootdisk -m d8ds /tmp/maintenance.img

-m before the medium makes the other article: the same boot and root,
with the repair tools and the diskette-to-diskette loop, and without
hdinit.  It boots to the same shell; nothing runs automatically.

The eight inch set is the install set plus fsck, label, login, the file
tools (ls, cp, mv, cat, mkdir), chg and fdj: 881 of 2250 blocks.  It
repairs an existing drive - fsck and label check and rewrite the
filesystem and label, format lays a drive down again, mount and the file
tools copy files on and off - and chg and fdj make and move diskettes.

The five inch maintenance article carries the repair set but not the
operator login, the diskette loop or cptree: fsck, label, format,
mount, the file tools and the shell, 733 of 760 blocks with 27 free.
Everything it carries is there for a repair; cptree copies whole trees
and is a system-making tool, not a repair one.

what is not here
----------------

	/.login		the shell reads .login and .profile; the
			diskette has neither, so booting it leaves you
			at the shell's "#" with nothing run
	era		a Whitesmiths command this tree has never built;
			the install uses rm -f where the original used it
	a compiler, sources, man pages, and the rest of /bin - a system
	with a hard disk installs those from the media that carry them

The old .sh file is gone too: it was the csh-like shell's startup
(`path /bin /usr/bin`), and the real shell has that path built in and
reads .login and .profile instead.

the label, and the format byte that used to be read
---------------------------------------------------

A hard sectored medium's geometry cannot be worked out from the drive;
the controller does not report it.  What says it now is the disk label:
mkbootimg writes one into the second half of block 0 - the block the
loader is in - and the kernel's dj.c reads it with dllabel() before it
reads anything else (sys/dj.c's djopen).  d5ds carries 40 cylinders,
two heads, ten sectors of 512 bytes, first sector 0.

The one thing the label cannot say is where it is.  Whether the boot
track numbers its sectors from 0 or 1 decides which sector block 0 is,
and that is the drive's to report rather than the medium's - the
characteristics byte's HARD bit - so djopen sets firstsec from the drive
and then reads the label with it.  The loader has no drive to ask, so it
takes the number from the label instead, which is why mkbootimg writes
it there.

A diskette with no label does not come up with a wrong geometry.  The
boot stops at the loader - "No disk label in the block this boot came
from" - and the kernel's own open leaves the drive mapping block 0 and
nothing else, so the first read past it is what fails rather than the
one at block 330 where the overlay modules are.

The eight inch media carry the same label and differ only in its
numbers: soft sectored, first sector 1, fifteen blocks to a track.
src/micronix/stand/boot/GNUmakefile's FLOPPIES table is what says so per
medium, and mkbootimg is what writes it.

